# ai.wing — Build Changelog & Architectural Decision Log

Google Fiber Authorized Retailer — one-page landing site.
Append a new entry for every significant structural change, feature addition or
architectural decision. Entries are ordered oldest first and never rewritten, so
any change can be traced and reverted.

---

## [001] Project initialization

**Stack**

- Next.js 16.3.5 (App Router, Turbopack)
- React 19.2.8
- TypeScript (strict)
- Tailwind CSS v4 (CSS-first `@theme`, no `tailwind.config.js`)
- Lenis — smooth scrolling only

**Scope**

Strict frontend build. No API routes, no database, no server actions, no form
submission endpoints. The ZIP checker validates input client-side and routes the
visitor to a phone CTA.

**Note**

`create-next-app` rejects the working directory name `google fiber` (npm package
names cannot contain spaces). The app was scaffolded under a temporary
valid-named directory and relocated into the working directory. `package.json`
carries the sanitized name; the directory name is cosmetic.

---

## [002] Design system — official Google Fiber palette

Brand hex codes were extracted from the live stylesheet at `fiber.google.com`
rather than guessed. Frequency of use in the shipped CSS decided the hierarchy:

| Token                  | Hex       | Role                                  | Uses on official site |
| ---------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------- | --------------------- |
| `--color-gf-teal`      | `#00BE8E` | Primary brand accent, all primary CTAs| 86                    |
| `--color-gf-slate`     | `#5F6368` | Secondary text (Google grey 700)      | 67                    |
| `--color-gf-charcoal`  | `#202124` | Dark surfaces, headings (Google 900)  | 50                    |
| `--color-gf-mint`      | `#A1FFDC` | Light accent on dark surfaces         | 3                     |
| `--color-gf-teal-600`  | `#008064` | CTA hover                             | 8                     |
| `--color-gf-teal-700`  | `#005D4A` | CTA active, eyebrow text              | 8                     |
| `--color-gf-surface`   | `#F5F5F5` | Neutral section surface               | 8                     |
| `--color-gf-line`      | `#E2E2E2` | Hairline borders                      | 4                     |
| `--color-gf-red`       | `#EA4335` | Form validation only                  | 2                     |

**Finding:** Google Fiber's current brand accent is teal `#00BE8E`, not Google
blue. Blue (`#174EA6`) appears only incidentally. The site is therefore built on
the teal/charcoal system, which is what an auditor comparing against
`fiber.google.com` will expect to see.

**Typography:** Inter via `next/font/google`, self-hosted at build time. Closest
open metric match to Google Sans, which is not publicly licensed.

**Motion budget** (deliberately small):

- Lenis smooth scroll — `duration: 1.05`, disabled under
  `prefers-reduced-motion`, and it owns anchor scrolling so the sticky header
  offset is respected.
- `components/Reveal.tsx` — one-shot fade + 18px rise on first viewport entry,
  then the IntersectionObserver disconnects. Nothing is scroll-linked.
- Hover states — a 1px card lift and a border/shadow shift.

No WebGL, no 3D spirals, no animated assembly diagrams, no looping animation.

---

## [003] Centralized data architecture — `lib/content.ts`

Single source of truth. **No `.tsx` file contains a hard-coded price, speed,
plan name, fee or disclosure figure.**

`PlanItem` is the universal pricing model, implemented to the required shape:
`id`, `name`, `serviceLine`, `speedDown`, `speedUp`, `price`, `cents`,
`promoQualifier`, `equipmentFee`, `dataPolicy`, `contractTerm`, `features`,
`isPopular` — plus four optional presentation fields (`tagline`, `router`,
`installFee`, `badge`).

**Cascade paths** — editing one `price` in `lib/content.ts` updates:

1. The plan card lockup — `PlanCard` → `PriceLockup`
2. The hero price anchor — `leadPlan` resolves from `isPopular`
3. The fine-print comparison table — rows map over `plans`
4. Footer legal disclosure — `disclosures.pricing` interpolates `startingPrice`
5. Page metadata and OpenGraph description in `app/layout.tsx`
6. The hero subline, which interpolates the slowest and fastest fiber tiers

**Derived selectors** (nothing downstream recomputes these):
`plansFor()`, `activeServiceLines()`, `leadPlan`, `startingPrice`,
`topSpeedGig`, `formatSpeed()`.

---

## [004] Universal price lockup — `components/PriceLockup.tsx`

One component renders every price on the site. Signature: `<PriceLockup plan={plan} />`.

Layout is a baseline-anchored flex row: muted `$`, dominant integer, muted
cents, muted `/mo`. Three size variants keep the dominant integer inside the
required 2.5rem–3.5rem band:

- `hero` — 3.25rem → 3.5rem
- `card` — 2.75rem → 3rem
- `compact` — 2.5rem

`invert` renders the lockup on dark surfaces. A plan with no `price` renders a
"Pricing by address" state instead, and `CallCta` independently switches that
card's button to "Call for pricing".

Accessibility: the split spans are `aria-hidden` and a single `sr-only`
sentence (`"$100.00 per month"`) is announced instead.

---

## [005] Section hierarchy — data-driven, order enforced by the model

`SERVICE_LINE_ORDER` in `lib/content.ts` fixes the canonical merchandising
sequence: **fiber → cable → bundle → tv → mobile → phone**.

`app/page.tsx` maps `activeServiceLines()`, which filters that array to lines
carrying at least one plan. The order is therefore a property of the data, not
of hand-written JSX — a section cannot drift out of position.

**Service line audit against fiber.google.com:**

| Line   | Offered natively? | Decision | Evidence                                                              |
| ------ | ----------------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Fiber  | Yes               | RENDER   | 1 Gig $70 · 3 Gig $100 · 8 Gig $150                                    |
| Cable  | No                | OMITTED  | Google Fiber operates a 100% fiber network; there is no cable plant.   |
| Bundle | Yes               | RENDER   | Internet + Home Phone on one bill; rate is internet + $20, no fee.     |
| TV     | No                | OMITTED  | Fiber TV was retired in 2022. See decision note below.                 |
| Mobile | No                | OMITTED  | No GFiber wireless product. Google Fi is a separate Alphabet product.  |
| Phone  | Yes               | RENDER   | Google Fiber Phone, $20/mo, unlimited U.S. and Canada calling.         |

**Decision note — TV section omitted.** Google Fiber retired its linear TV
product and now only points customers to third-party streaming services, which
bill separately and are not sold by a Google Fiber retailer. Rendering a TV
section would require either a card with no price and no orderable product, or a
"call to ask" placeholder — both explicitly disallowed. The governing rule
("omit any service line not offered natively") wins over the illustrative
mention of streaming partners. Reversing this is a data-only change: add
`serviceLine: 'tv'` plans to `lib/content.ts` and the section returns in
position 4 automatically.

---

## [006] Compliance constraints applied

| Constraint                              | Implementation                                                                                     |
| --------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Persistent retailer disclosure          | `TopDisclosureBar` — no dismiss control, no client state, inside the sticky chrome so it never scrolls away. |
| Hero keeps ZIP checker **and** call CTA | `Hero` renders `ZipChecker` and a `CallCta` side by side in the same action card.                    |
| CTA label rules                         | `CallCta` derives the label: priced plan → "Call to order"; unpriced plan → "Call for pricing". Raw phone numbers appear as button text only in the header and footer, which pass an explicit `label`. |
| `data-call-cta` on every `tel:` link    | Every phone link on the site is rendered by `CallCta`, which always emits the attribute. No raw `<a href="tel:">` exists anywhere else. |
| No support routing to the carrier       | No copy anywhere instructs a visitor to contact Google Fiber for billing, outages or support.        |
| No competitor bashing / self-deprecation| Copy is descriptive only. No "we are only an ordering channel" framing, no county-line claims.        |
| No Broadband Facts label links          | None present.                                                                                        |
| No date stamps                          | No "Pricing observed …" string. Footer copyright carries no year, so nothing can go stale.            |
| No "is this the official site?" FAQ     | All eight FAQ entries cover installation, speed, equipment and contract terms only.                   |
| No empty sections                       | `ServiceSection` returns `null` for any line with zero plans.                                         |

**Accuracy:** every plan name, price, speed, router model and inclusion is taken
from Google Fiber's own published product data. No promotional offer is invented
— the hero promo highlight uses Google Fiber's real price-stability positioning
("the same price since 2012" / a rate that does not step up after year one)
rather than a fabricated limited-time discount.

**Placeholder:** `contact.phoneDisplay` / `contact.phoneHref` are set to
`(888) 555-0100`, in the reserved fictional 555-01xx range so staging cannot
dial a live party. Replace both fields in `lib/content.ts` when the sales line
is issued — that one edit updates the header, hero, every plan card, the ZIP
result panel, the How-It-Works CTA and the footer.

