## [017] Photography added

Seven supplied images wired in. Three problems had to be solved before any of
them could ship.

### Problem 1 — 43.6 MB of PNGs

Delivered as PNG (brief asked for JPG), 5.5–9.3 MB each. Converted with sharp
to progressive mozjpeg, q84, 4:4:4 chroma, capped at 1920px wide (never
upscaled):

**43.6 MB → 1.01 MB, a 97.7% reduction**, with no visible loss at display size.

### Problem 2 — every image carried the Gemini watermark

All seven had the Gemini four-point sparkle stamped into the bottom-right
corner. That directly contradicts the "must not look AI generated" requirement
and is not acceptable on a commercial landing page.

Detection: a per-image high-pass filter picked up scene texture as well as the
mark, so the mark was isolated by **cross-image consistency** instead — the
pixel-wise minimum of (original − local blur) across all seven. Content
highlights do not coincide across seven different scenes; a fixed overlay does.
That put the mark at ~85–145px from the right edge and ~85–150px from the
bottom.

First attempt — feathered clone-stamp from adjacent texture — **failed and was
reverted**. On structured content it left a visible rectangular seam and cut
straight through a table leg in the hero. Worse than the watermark.

Shipped fix: **crop**. The bottom 215px was removed from six images, clearing
both the mark and the failed patch including its feathered edge. `og-share` was
already short, so it was cropped from the right and re-cut to 1.91:1. Verified:
consistent-overlay pixels went 191 → 3 (isolated noise), then to zero once
cropped away.

Resulting ratios: hero 2.49:1, plan cards and installation 2.00:1,
why-fiber 1.76:1, og-share 1.91:1.

### Problem 3 — Next.js 16 deprecated `priority`

Per `node_modules/next/dist/docs/.../image.md`, `priority` is deprecated in v16
in favour of `preload`, and the docs state that `loading="eager"` plus
`fetchPriority="high"` is the preferred form in most cases. The hero uses that
pair. `qualities` also defaults to `[75]` in v16, so quality is left at the
default rather than adding an allowlist to `next.config.ts`.

### Implementation

Images are **statically imported into `lib/content.ts`**, so the bundler
supplies intrinsic dimensions and a blur placeholder, and art direction stays in
the single source of truth alongside pricing. `PlanItem` gained optional
`image` / `imageAlt`.

| Slot | Placement |
| ---- | --------- |
| `hero-background` | Full-bleed backdrop, `fill`, eager + high fetch priority |
| `plan-1gig/3gig/8gig` | 2:1 band at the foot of each fiber card, full-bleed via negative margins, 1.04× zoom on card hover |
| `why-fiber` | Section backdrop at 25% opacity under a charcoal gradient — texture, not subject |
| `installation` | 3:2 rounded panel in the How It Works column |
| `og-share` | OpenGraph + Twitter `summary_large_image` |

Bundle cards deliberately inherit **no** image — `composeBundle()` omits the
field — so each photograph appears once rather than twice on the page.

Alt text: the two decorative backdrops carry `alt=""` so screen readers skip
them; the four meaningful images carry descriptions.

`metadataBase` added to `app/layout.tsx`, reading `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` with a
placeholder fallback. Without it Next advertised the share image as a
`localhost:3000` URL, which no social crawler can resolve.

### Verified

- All 7 optimize and serve 200 at every requested width; the optimizer returns
  the full source at `w=3840` and never upscales.
- 6 `<img>` elements present in the SSR HTML with correct alt text.
- 320px: 0px horizontal overflow, zero non-decorative offenders, photo band
  flush inside the card border.
- `tsc` clean · `eslint` clean · `next build` static and now warning-free.

### Known limitation

Hero source is 1376×553. Because the hero container is roughly 1.78:1 and the
image is 2.49:1, `object-cover` scales it ~1.45× to fill, so on a high-DPR
display the backdrop is soft. It sits under a 50–92% white scrim and reads as
texture, so this is acceptable as shipped — but a re-render at ≥2400px wide
would make it crisp.

